{"type":"message","id":"21f775c3","parentId":"dbc46ddd","timestamp":"2026-05-18T10:08:38.400Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_pJxn1AFcgRfGPVzonvrIgpbH","name":"read","arguments":{"path":"/tmp/research.json"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"local-bifrost","model":"gpt-5.4-mini","usage":{"input":11590,"output":213,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":11803,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1779098915863,"responseId":"chatcmpl-DgpBnNOGjLnYnDY8xkeaCVO4kf1pD"}}
{"type":"message","id":"1cc89f46","parentId":"21f775c3","timestamp":"2026-05-18T10:08:38.417Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_pJxn1AFcgRfGPVzonvrIgpbH","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"{\n  \"status\": \"ok\",\n  \"story_id\": \"bitcoin-depot-bankruptcy-2026-05-18\",\n  \"topic_theme\": \"Bitcoin Depot Files for Chapter 11 Bankruptcy\",\n  \"primary_keyword\": \"Bitcoin Depot\",\n  \"primary_headline\": \"Bitcoin Depot, North America\\u2019s largest bitcoin ATM operator, files for Chapter 11 bankruptcy\",\n  \"primary_asset\": \"Bitcoin\",\n  \"chart_coin\": \"bitcoin\",\n  \"sources_used\": [\n    \"CoinDesk\",\n    \"Decrypt\"\n  ],\n  \"source_urls\": [\n    \"https://www.coindesk.com/markets/2026/05/18/bitcoin-depot-once-north-america-s-largest-bitcoin-atm-operator-files-for-bankruptcy\",\n    \"https://decrypt.co/368146/crypto-atm-operator-bitcoin-depot-files-for-chapter-11-bankruptcy\"\n  ],\n  \"combined_key_facts\": [\n    \"Bitcoin Depot filed for Chapter 11 bankruptcy protection in Texas federal court and said it is beginning an orderly wind-down and sale process.\",\n    \"The company operated more than 9,000 Bitcoin ATM locations across North America before shutting down its network.\",\n    \"CEO Alex Holmes said the business became unsustainable amid stricter compliance requirements, transaction limits, state bans or restrictions in some jurisdictions, and rising litigation and enforcement costs.\",\n    \"Decrypt reported a 49.2% year-over-year revenue decline in Q1 2026, along with a $9.5 million net loss versus $12.2 million in net income a year earlier.\",\n    \"Bitcoin Depot\\u2019s stock had fallen 79.48% over the previous six months, and the company also faced a reported $3.7 million theft from its crypto wallets plus legal pressure in Canada.\"\n  ],\n  \"aggregated_raw_content\": \"Bitcoin Depot, long known as North America\\u2019s largest Bitcoin ATM operator, has filed for Chapter 11 bankruptcy protection and is shutting down its entire machine network. CoinDesk identified the company as North America\\u2019s largest bitcoin ATM operator and said it filed for bankruptcy on May 18, 2026. Decrypt\\u2019s reporting added that the firm operated more than 9,000 Bitcoin ATM locations across North America before the closure. The bankruptcy filing landed in Texas federal court, and the company said the process is intended to support an orderly wind-down of operations and a sale of the company\\u2019s assets.\\n\\nCEO Alex Holmes blamed an increasingly hostile regulatory environment for the collapse. In Decrypt\\u2019s account, Holmes said the regulatory framework for Bitcoin ATM operators has \\u201cshifted significantly,\\u201d leaving the company\\u2019s business model unsustainable. He pointed to growing compliance obligations, including new transaction limits, plus outright restrictions or bans in some jurisdictions. Holmes also said operators have faced increasing litigation and regulatory enforcement, which materially affected Bitcoin Depot\\u2019s business and financial position. The filing will be overseen by the U.S. Bankruptcy Court for the Southern District of Texas, and the company expects separate restructuring proceedings in Canada for its Canadian entities.\\n\\nThe company\\u2019s financials had already been deteriorating before the bankruptcy. Decrypt reported that Bitcoin Depot\\u2019s Q1 2026 revenue fell 49.2% year over year. The company posted a $9.5 million net loss in the quarter, compared with $12.2 million in net income a year earlier. Investor confidence had also collapsed: Decrypt said the stock had dropped 79.48% over the prior six months as regulatory uncertainty intensified and the business weakened. Earlier reports also indicated that the company had been warning about bankruptcy risk as ATM revenue fell and legal pressure increased.\\n\\nBitcoin Depot\\u2019s troubles were not isolated to revenue. Decrypt said the company overhauled leadership in March 2026, appointing Holmes as CEO after Connecticut suspended its money transmission license. A month later, the company disclosed that hackers had breached its IT systems and stolen $3.7 million from its crypto wallets. The firm was also dealing with ongoing legal battles involving an $18.5 million award dispute tied to its Canadian subsidiary. Those compounding pressures helped push the operator toward court-supervised restructuring.\\n\\nThe filing lands in a broader period of strain for the Bitcoin ATM sector. Decrypt noted that Tennessee became the second state to outlaw Bitcoin ATMs after Indiana in April, while Canadian authorities have also proposed restrictions. The company\\u2019s collapse underscores how quickly a business built on consumer access to cash-to-Bitcoin kiosks can be squeezed by tightening rules, enforcement action, and declining transaction economics. What once looked like a high-growth retail gateway for Bitcoin adoption is now colliding with a much harsher compliance and liability environment.\\n\\nCoinDesk\\u2019s headline framed the event as a bankruptcy filing by the company that was once North America\\u2019s largest bitcoin ATM operator. Decrypt\\u2019s more detailed coverage made clear how severe the unwind has become: a full shutdown of more than 9,000 machines, a Chapter 11 process in Texas, planned Canadian proceedings, a sharp revenue drop, a quarterly loss after prior profitability, a major stock decline, and additional legal and security setbacks. Together, the two reports show a single major crypto business event centered on Bitcoin infrastructure, regulation, and the viability of the cash-to-crypto ATM model in 2026.\"\n}"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1779098918417}}
